namespace molpro{
namespace linalg{
/*!
@page TopicSpaces Spaces

The iterative solvers work by expanding the solution in a small subspace of the full vector space, solving the problem
projected onto that subspace, and using the residual to propose new expansion vectors. The vectors involved are
organised into several (sub)spaces. Their names appear throughout the code (e.g. `nP`, `nQ`, `nD`, `cparamsq()`,
`handlers.rq()`) and are described here.

| Space | Container type | Owned by    | Content                                                                      |
|-------|----------------|-------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| R     | `R`            | user        | working-set vectors: current solutions, residuals and new expansion vectors |
| P     | `P`            | solver      | sparse vectors spanning a few selected coordinates of the full space        |
| Q     | `Q`            | solver      | history of previous expansion vectors and their actions                      |
| D     | `Q`            | solver      | compressed remainder of deleted Q vectors needed to reproduce the solutions |
| X     | –              | solver      | the full subspace P+Q+D in which the subspace problem is solved              |

@section SpacesContainers The container types R, Q and P

`IterativeSolver<R, Q, P>` is templated on three container types, one for each kind of space (see also
@ref TopicArrayHandlers).

- `R` is the container for the working-set vectors. These are typically held in memory and are created by the
  client program; `IterativeSolver` never creates `R` vectors itself.
- `Q` is the container for all other full-length vectors that the solver has to keep, i.e. the Q and D spaces and the
  right-hand-side vectors of linear equations. As there can be many of them they are typically held on backing store
  and/or distributed across processes. The solver creates them as copies of `R` vectors.
- `P` specifies a single P-space vector, which is a strictly sparse vector in the underlying space, e.g.
  `std::map<size_t, double>` mapping a coordinate index to its coefficient.

Operations between vectors of different spaces are performed by the corresponding array handler of
`ArrayHandlers<R, Q, P>`: `rr()`, `qq()`, `pp()`, `rq()`, `rp()`, `qr()` and `qp()`. For example, the overlap of a
new R vector with a Q vector is calculated with `handlers.rq().dot(r, q)`.

@section SpacesR R space (working set)

The R space consists of the vectors that are exchanged between the solver and the user in every iteration: the user
passes the current parameters and their actions (linear problems) or residuals (non-linear problems) to
`add_vector()`, preconditions the returned residuals, and gets back from `end_iteration()` the new parameters for which
the action has to be calculated next. There is one R vector per root in the *working set*, i.e. per root that has
not converged yet (`IterativeSolver::working_set()`).

R vectors are not stored by the solver between iterations. When a new set of R parameters is added to the subspace,
copies of the parameters and their actions are made in the Q space (`IXSpace::update_qspace()`).

Before new R parameters are proposed (`detail::propose_rspace()`), the preconditioned residuals are normalised,
orthogonalised against P+Q+D and each other, and any that turn out to be linearly dependent are removed. The
thresholds for this are in `RSpaceOptions`:

- `norm_thresh`: vectors with a norm below this are considered null,
- `svd_thresh`: smallest singular value of the overlap matrix allowed when constructing the working set.

@section SpacesP P space

The P space is spanned by a small number of sparse vectors, typically unit vectors along selected coordinates of the
full space. For these the kernel matrix is not applied through `action()`. Instead, the user provides the matrix
projected onto the P space (`Problem::pp_action_matrix()`) and a function evaluating the action of the matrix on
vectors that are linear combinations of P vectors (`Problem::p_action()`). This allows the part of the problem that
is most important (e.g. the coordinates with the lowest diagonal elements in an eigenvalue problem) to be treated
exactly from the start.

- The P space can only be used with linear problems with hermitian kernels.
- It must be set up while the subspace is still empty, i.e. before the first call to `add_vector()`, by calling
  `add_p()`. Its size must be zero or at least the number of roots sought.
- When solving through `IterativeSolver::solve()` and the problem provides `diagonals()`, a P space is selected
  automatically from the coordinates with the lowest diagonal elements. Its size is limited by `max_p`, and only
  coordinates whose diagonal element lies within `p_threshold` of the lowest one are included.
- `suggest_p()` proposes coordinates that would be good candidates for extending the P space.

@section SpacesQ Q space

The Q space holds the history of previous expansion vectors (*Q parameters*) together with their actions, both
stored in `Q` containers (`subspace::QSpace`). New parameters are prepended, so the most recent vectors come first.
New parameters that are linearly dependent among themselves are discarded when they are added.

In the linear solvers the size of the Q space can be controlled through `QSpaceOptions` (exposed as solver options):

- `max_size_qspace`: maximum number of Q vectors. If the Q space grows beyond this, the vectors with the smallest
  contribution to any of the current solutions are deleted and replaced by a D space (see @ref SpacesD).
- `min_size_qspace`: deletions that would shrink the Q space below this size are cancelled.
- `contrib_thresh`: Q vectors whose largest contribution to any current solution is smaller than this are considered
  redundant and deleted even if the Q space is not full.

The non-linear solvers (`NonLinearEquationsDIIS`, `OptimizeBFGS`) also store their history in the Q space and have a
`max_size_qspace` option, but delete old vectors directly without building a D space.

@section SpacesD D space

Deleting Q vectors from the subspace would change the solutions of the subspace problem and lose the progress made so
far. To prevent this, the linear Davidson solvers keep a D space (`subspace::DSpace`), which stores the complement of
the P+Q+R subspace that is needed to reproduce the previous solutions exactly.

When Q vectors are deleted (`detail::construct_dspace()`), the current solutions are projected onto the space spanned
by the deleted Q vectors and the old D vectors. After removing null and linearly dependent combinations, the
projections are normalised and become the new D space; the old D space is discarded. D vectors and their actions are
stored in `Q` containers. As a result the D space never holds more vectors than there are solutions, and the
subspace still contains the previous solutions, even though the Q vectors that contributed to them are gone.

Because the D space is built from linear combinations of stored vectors, numerical noise accumulates in it over many
iterations. It can therefore be reset periodically (`reset_D` option, `detail::DSpaceResetter`): the full current
solutions are moved into the Q space as new parameters, the Q vectors that overlap most with them are deleted, and
the D space is cleared. If the working set is smaller than the number of solutions, the reset is spread over several
iterations. The size of the Q space after a reset can be limited separately with `reset_D_max_Q_size`.

@section SpacesX X space (full subspace)

The X space is the union of the P, Q and D spaces, in which the subspace problem is solved
(`subspace::IXSpace`, `subspace::XSpace`). It owns the containers of the individual spaces as well as the equation
data projected onto the subspace (`subspace::SubspaceData`):

- `EqnData::H`: the kernel matrix (for DIIS the matrix of residual overlaps),
- `EqnData::S`: the overlap matrix,
- `EqnData::rhs`: the projected right-hand-side vectors (linear equations only).

The rows and columns of these matrices are ordered as P, then Q, then D. The sizes and offsets of the three blocks are
given by `subspace::Dimensions`:

@code{.unparsed}
 0          oQ()=nP      oD()=nP+nQ        nX()=nP+nQ+nD
 |---- P ----|----- Q -----|------ D ------|
@endcode

The subspace solver (`subspace::ISubspaceSolver`) solves the projected problem in the X space and returns the
solutions as coefficient matrices. The full solutions and residuals are then constructed in R containers as linear
combinations of the P, Q and D vectors (for P vectors through `p_action()`).

@section SpacesIteration Overview of an iteration

For the linear Davidson solvers, a single iteration involves the spaces as follows:

1. The user evaluates the action of the kernel on the current R parameters and calls `add_vector()`.
2. The R parameters and actions are copied into the Q space, and the equation data of the X space is extended.
3. The subspace problem is solved in X = P+Q+D, and solutions and residuals are constructed in the R vectors.
4. The user preconditions the residuals and calls `end_iteration()`.
5. If the Q space is too large, Q vectors are deleted and a new D space is constructed from the current solutions
   (or the D space is reset if requested).
6. The preconditioned residuals are orthonormalised against P+Q+D and returned as the new R parameters.

*/
}
}
